The <b>Vehicle Routing Problem</b> (VRP) algorithm assigns jobs to a fleet of vehicles and puts each vehicle's jobs in order, solved by <a href="https://github.com/VROOM-Project/vroom">VROOM</a> on a Valhalla time and distance matrix. Install <i>pyvroom</i> in the plugin settings first.

<b>Jobs</b> are a point layer of stops. Optional fields set each job's service time (seconds), delivery and pickup amounts, a time window, required skills and a priority (0 to 100).
<b>Vehicles</b> are a point layer of where each vehicle starts. Optional fields set its travel mode (auto, truck, bus, bicycle, pedestrian, motorcycle — empty uses this algorithm's), capacity, shift, skills, maximum number of jobs and one break (window and duration in seconds). Vehicles return to their start unless you untick it.
<b>Vehicle end points</b> (advanced): a point layer of where vehicles finish instead, each with the ID of its vehicle (at most one per vehicle). A vehicle with an end point finishes there whether or not vehicles return to their start.
<b>Costs</b> (advanced): the solver serves as many jobs as it can, then keeps the total cost lowest. A vehicle's cost is its <i>fixed cost</i> (if it's used at all) + <i>cost per hour</i> × hours travelled + <i>cost per km</i> × km. The defaults (0, 3600, 0) make the cost the travel time in seconds. A fixed cost makes the solver use fewer vehicles, a cost per km prefers shorter routes over faster ones, and different costs per vehicle prefer the cheaper vehicles. The <i>routes</i> output has each route's <i>cost</i>.
<b>Driving limits</b> (advanced): a vehicle's <i>maximum travel time</i> (seconds) and <i>maximum distance</i> (meters) count only driving, not service or waiting. Jobs that would break them go to another vehicle or stay unassigned.

<b>Mixed fleets</b>: each travel mode gets its own Valhalla matrix and route lines. The costing options in <i>Advanced Parameters</i> only apply to this algorithm's own travel mode; other vehicles use Valhalla's defaults.
<b>Pickup and delivery</b> (advanced): two jobs with the same <i>shipment ID</i>, one with role <i>pickup</i> and one <i>delivery</i>, are carried by one vehicle, pickup first. The shipment's amount is the delivery amount of either job.
<b>Assigned vehicle</b> (advanced): keeps a job on that vehicle (its vehicle ID) and re-optimises everything else, e.g. after a change of plan.
<b>Keep the order</b> (advanced): with the assigned vehicle and <i>sequence</i> fields set, the routes are kept exactly as given and only their times are computed. Jobs without a vehicle or sequence are unassigned; anything the order breaks (late arrivals, overloads) is listed in the <i>violations</i> fields (<i>lead_time</i> = the vehicle would have to leave before its shift starts to make a time window). Vehicles without jobs get no route. Breaks are left out.

Amounts can have several dimensions as comma separated numbers, e.g. "500, 2" for kilograms and pallets; all jobs and vehicles must use the same order. Skills are comma separated integers: a job only goes to a vehicle with all its skills.
Times are seconds, "HH:MM[:SS]" text, or time / date-time fields (from midnight / the Unix epoch). Use one kind for all times in a run.

Outputs:
  1. <i>routes</i>: one line per used vehicle with its travel mode, number of jobs, travel duration and distance, service and waiting time, cost, start and end time,
  2. <i>stops</i>: every step in order (start, jobs, breaks, end) with arrival, departure, waiting and load after the step,
  3. <i>unassigned</i>: jobs no vehicle could take: <i>unreachable</i> on the network, <i>not assigned</i> by the solver (capacity, time windows, skills) or, when keeping the order, <i>no vehicle or sequence</i>.

The matrix and the route lines are split into requests within your Valhalla server's limits (<i>Advanced Parameters</i>): 2500 matrix pairs and 20 route locations per request are Valhalla's defaults. If the server allows fewer (the public FOSSGIS server allows 100 matrix pairs), it says so in its error and the requests are automatically redone within its limit, with a warning in the log. VROOM uses one fixed matrix, so travel times don't change with the time of day.
<b>Points far from roads</b>: Valhalla serves every point from its nearest road, even kilometers away, so a point in the wrong place (or a layer in the wrong CRS) still looks served. The log warns about every job, vehicle, end point or landfill more than 500 m from a road, with its distance (<i>Advanced Parameters</i>, 0 = don't check: it costs a few small requests).
